From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent 977: only one genconfig at a time

"multiap conf" is reached from two places that fire together on the first boot
after an upgrade: /etc/uci-defaults/991-multiap-genconfig, and the ieee80211
hotplug handler, which runs once per radio - three times on MT7996. Nothing
serialised them, and map_genconf() reads /etc/config/mapagent, rewrites it and
commits. Two concurrent runs read the same starting state, and the one that
commits second drops whatever the first one added.

Measured on hardware 2026-07-31, colocated controller, boot after flash:

  up=15s  multiap <- S10boot       :: uci set mapagent.bhmld_ap_5=ap ...
  up=16s  multiap <- hotplug-call  :: uci set mapagent.bhmld_ap_5=ap ...

  before: ap=8  bhmld=4  mld=2  affiliated_ap=15
  after:  ap=6  bhmld=0  mld=2  affiliated_ap=13

The trace was taken with a shim in front of uci that records every writing
invocation together with its caller, and it contains no "uci delete" for those
sections at all - in either run. Nothing removes them. The write is lost.

That was confirmed by making the write impossible rather than by watching it:
a read-only bind mount over /etc/config/mapagent held from the 20th to the 90th
second turned the silent overwrite into "/sbin/uci: I/O error" and the
configuration came through the flash intact, ap=8 bhmld=4, where the same flash
without it ended at ap=6 bhmld=0.

The same race accounts for the bsta sections that outlive their interfaces. A
lost write drops an addition in one direction and a removal in the other, so the
leftovers are not generated - they are forgotten, which is why they neither
accumulate nor disappear. Their count differs per node under an identical image
and an identical role: three on one agent, one on another. Deliberate generation
would produce the same count on both.

A plain reboot is unaffected, and this is worth stating because it shaped the
whole investigation: by then uci-defaults have been removed, only the hotplug
path runs, and there is nobody to race with. Ten reboots across the same day
never reproduced it; every flash did.

The lock is held across the whole generator rather than just the commit. The
read and the commit have to be one unit - serialising only the write would still
let the second run begin from a stale copy and reintroduce the same loss. The
pattern and the lock directory are the ones this script already uses for the
backhaul path.

What this does not address: map-agent writes to the same file from its own
process. That is a third writer, it is not on this path, and if it turns out to
lose writes as well it needs its own answer rather than a wider lock here.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

--- a/src/script/multiap	2026-07-31 15:27:00.840991915 +0200
+++ b/src/script/multiap	2026-07-31 15:27:00.841101129 +0200
@@ -102,7 +102,46 @@
 setup_conf() {
 	. /lib/multiap/map_genconfig
 
-	map_genconf
+	# Only one generator at a time.
+	#
+	# This entry point is reached from two places that fire together on the
+	# first boot after an upgrade: /etc/uci-defaults/991-multiap-genconfig,
+	# and the ieee80211 hotplug handler, which runs once per radio - three
+	# times on MT7996. Nothing serialised them.
+	#
+	# map_genconf() reads /etc/config/mapagent, rewrites it and commits. Two
+	# concurrent runs therefore both read the same starting state, and the one
+	# that commits second silently drops whatever the first one added. Nothing
+	# is deleted - the write is simply lost.
+	#
+	# Measured on hardware 2026-07-31, colocated controller, boot after flash:
+	#
+	#   up=15s  multiap <- S10boot       :: uci set mapagent.bhmld_ap_5=ap ...
+	#   up=16s  multiap <- hotplug-call  :: uci set mapagent.bhmld_ap_5=ap ...
+	#   result: ap 8 -> 6, bhmld 4 -> 0, affiliated_ap 15 -> 13
+	#
+	# and there is no "uci delete" anywhere in the trace for those sections.
+	# Blocking the file read-only across that window (bind mount, EROFS) left
+	# the configuration intact, which is what identified the write as lost
+	# rather than undone.
+	#
+	# The same race explains the leftover bsta sections that outlive their
+	# interfaces: a lost write drops an addition in one direction and a removal
+	# in the other. Their count differs per node under an identical image -
+	# three on one agent, one on another - which is what a race looks like and
+	# what deliberate generation does not.
+	#
+	# A plain reboot is unaffected: uci-defaults have been removed by then, so
+	# only the hotplug path runs and there is nobody to race with.
+	#
+	# The lock is held across the whole generator, because the read and the
+	# commit have to be one unit - serialising only the commit would still let
+	# the second run start from a stale copy. Same pattern and same directory
+	# as the backhaul lock already used in this script.
+	(
+		flock -x 200
+		map_genconf
+	) 200>/var/lock/map.genconfig.lock
 }
 
 bsta_updown() {
